#!/usr/bin/env bash
# vgshell: the VGS shell runner and plugin manager CLI.
#
# User-facing help is in help_text below. This header holds developer notes.
#
# `start` takes no arguments. It selects the Hyprland instance, checks the
# runtime floor, serializes with $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/vgshell-start.lock, asks
# Hyprland to launch `vgshell run` with its output in the state log, and
# returns after the guarded shell answers, so the caller's terminal is free.
#
# `run` takes no arguments: the shell always runs as this process's child,
# and this process stays in the foreground and supervises it. A shell that
# exits with a non-zero status while Hyprland answers starts again after
# the next delay of the supervision table below, and each relaunch prints
# `vgshell: shell=exited status=<n> relaunch=<k> delay=<s> session=<locked|unlocked|unknown>`
# on stderr. A run as long as the table's healthy run starts the count
# again. The exit after the last delay gives up with
# `vgshell: shell=gave-up exits=<n> status=<n>` and a Hyprland notice, unless
# the session is not read as unlocked: then every later exit relaunches
# after the last delay. A shell that exits 0, a Hyprland that no longer
# answers (`vgshell: shell=exited status=<n> hyprland=gone`) and HUP, INT or
# TERM to this process, which stops the shell, end this process with the
# shell's status.
# Before anything else it checks Quickshell, a running
# Hyprland, node, python3 and git against the floor its preflight table
# holds, and refuses
# with `vgshell: refused: preflight=<tool> have=<version|none|unknown> need=<version|present>`
# and exit 78 on the first tool below it. This process holds the instance
# lock, $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/vgshell.lock, until it exits; the file holds the
# shell's pid, which is the identity the shell's guard checks, and is empty
# from the shell's exit until this process starts the next one. No process
# the shell starts holds the lock. Every command that contacts the shell
# addresses that instance alone, so a shell started any other way is never
# reached. `plugin validate`, `plugin add`,
# `plugin update`, `plugin remove`, `plugin outdated`, `plugin requirements`,
# `plugin settings` and `doctor` work with no shell running.
#
# `restart` checks the same floor first, so below it restart refuses as run
# does and leaves the running shell alone. It refuses while the session is
# locked, because the shell owns the lock client. Otherwise it sends TERM to
# the runner, the parent of the pid in the instance lock when that parent
# holds the lock, which stops the shell and exits with no relaunch, or to
# the pid itself when its parent holds no lock; waits for that lock to free,
# which it does when the runner exits after the shell, and uses start's
# Hyprland launch path. It prints the new pid once that shell answers as the
# guarded instance, and refuses
# `start=exited pid=<pid|none>` once that run has written the lock file
# and no process holds the lock any more: the new runner ended with no
# guarded shell, the pid the last one the file named.
#
# add, update and remove run no code from the plugin and no git hook, and ask
# for no privilege: installed plugins live under
# ${XDG_CONFIG_HOME:-~/.config}/vgshell/plugins/<id>. add lands a plugin
# with a bar widget enabled, its widget in its default bar section, so a
# running shell builds its code at once, and lands any other plugin
# disabled; it says which before it asks `vgshell: add <id>? [y/N]`.
# Without a url, add asks for one with gum input when stdin is a
# terminal, the prompt the core floating TUI `core/plugin-add` shows, and
# otherwise refuses with url=missing and exit 2; an empty answer refuses
# the same way, a cancelled prompt with cancelled=url and a system without
# gum with gum=missing. update prints the incoming diff, then asks
# `vgshell: update <id>? [y/N]` on stderr and fast-forwards only on y or yes;
# remove asks `vgshell: remove <id>? [y/N]` before it deletes. Any other
# answer refuses with declined=<id>. With no terminal on stdin each
# refuses with no-terminal=<id> instead, unless --yes gives the answer.
# A refused add installs nothing, a refused update leaves the checkout at
# its old commit and a refused remove leaves the plugin installed. Each ends with
# `shell=rescan-started` or `shell=rescan-queued` when a shell runs (the
# scan's result is in `vgshell plugin list`), or `shell=not-running` when none
# does and the next start reads the change. `plugin rescan` prints the same
# line alone. add asks the shell for that scan through `pluginInstalled
# <id>`, so the shell also shows its requirement notice for the new plugin.
#
# After that line add prints `requires <name> (<package>)` for each
# requirement of the new plugin that the scan did not find, the package
# being this system's pick as `plugin list` names it, then one
# `install: vgshell pkg run install [--manager <id>] <package>...` line per
# manager those packages come from; a manager the package table gives no
# install steps, nix, gets `by-hand <manager> <package>...` instead, for the
# user to add to that system's own configuration. On a terminal it then asks
# `Install now? [y/N]` on stderr and on y or yes runs each command in turn,
# its exit status the first failing one's; any other answer, or no
# terminal on stdin, leaves the requirements printed for the user. The report
# reads the manifest, PATH and D-Bus names only, so add still runs no plugin code.
# `vgshell pkg run` asks a running shell to rescan once its steps end, so the
# shell reports the installed requirements present.
#
# `plugin requirements` prints one line per requirement of the plugin the
# shell would hold under <id>, enabled or not:
# `<state> <name>[ (<package>)][ optional]: <purpose>`, state `present`,
# `missing` or `outdated`, judged as the shell's requirement notice judges
# it: a present command with a floor of the core or an enabled plugin has
# its version read and its holder looked up as the shell does, so a command
# below its floor is `outdated` and the package named is the one its
# holder's manager provides; a command whose version cannot be read, and
# every command of a disabled plugin, stays `present`. `doctor` prints the same
# lines, each led by `core` or the plugin's id, for config/requirements.json
# and every plugin the configuration enables, then one line, `session <present|missing|unknown>
# uwsm-managed: <text>`, which says whether the session is uwsm-managed,
# read from the user manager's graphical-session.target; an unmanaged one
# names what breaks and the entry to log in with, "Hyprland
# (uwsm-managed)". --json prints the rows as
# one JSON line, each `{ name, bus, packages, optional, purpose, floor,
# state, package }` with package `{ manager, name }` or null and floor the
# lowest version the plugin works with or null: a list for `plugin
# requirements`, and `{ core: [rows], plugins: { <id>: [rows] }, session:
# { graphicalSession } }` for `doctor`. Both read the plugin directories,
# PATH, D-Bus names and versions as the shell's scan does, and change
# nothing.
#
# `plugin settings` prints the settings the plugin the shell would hold
# under <id> hands its service, panel and floating TUIs: its manifest's
# defaults under its plugins[] row in the shipped and user shell.json, as
# one JSON object on one line. A value the plugin's schema refuses is
# refused as setting=<key> want=<type> id=<id>, and an id no accepted
# manifest names as unknown=<id>. It reads and changes nothing else.
#
# `plugin outdated` and `theme outdated` fetch every installed checkout
# with no hook, no prompt and no askpass dialog, each fetch ended at 10 s,
# and change nothing else: no merge, no checkout, no FETCH_HEAD, no plugin
# or package code. Each prints one row per checkout, sorted by id:
# `<id> behind=<n> head=<commit> upstream=<commit>` with 12-digit commits,
# or `<id> error=<refusal>`: the keyed line update prints for the same
# cause, or fetch=<id> timeout=10s. --json prints the rows as one JSON
# line, `[{ id, behind, head, upstream, error }]` with full commit ids,
# `[]` when nothing is installed; when error is set the other three are
# null. Error rows still exit 0. `theme outdated` holds the theme lock
# shared: a second outdated runs beside it. It waits up to 10 s for an
# exclusive holder, then refuses with outdated=themes reason=busy.
#
# Theme packages are the shipped themes/<name>/ and the installed
# ${XDG_CONFIG_HOME:-~/.config}/vgshell/themes/<name>/; an installed package
# hides a shipped one of its name, except the reserved `vgs`. `theme apply`
# holds flock on ${XDG_CONFIG_HOME:-~/.config}/vgshell/theme.lock for the whole
# apply, so a second apply from any caller is refused with reason=busy. It
# renders the package and every target under themes/targets/ whose commands
# are on PATH and that shell.json's disabledTargets does not list into
# ${XDG_STATE_HOME:-~/.local/state}/vgshell/theme/, writes theme.name beside it,
# keeps each target's include line in its application's configuration file
# (a disabled target's is removed; one that renders nothing keeps the file it
# landed before), then copies the package's theme.json byte for byte to
# theme.json beside the lock, the file the shell watches. No other
# application file is written. Last, each landed target whose bytes changed
# or whose reload is pending runs its target.json reload hook, bounded by
# its timeout, with output discarded and no lock descriptor; a hook that
# fails or times out leaves the target reload-pending in
# ${XDG_STATE_HOME:-~/.local/state}/vgshell/reload-pending.json. `theme reload`
# holds the same lock and runs every pending hook again, and nothing else.
# Apply records the package's name, the hash of the bytes it wrote to the
# theme file, a digest of the package's content and the user's terminal
# font, when shell.json sets one, in
# ${XDG_STATE_HOME:-~/.local/state}/vgshell/applied.json. `theme follow` holds
# the same lock, first waiting up to 10 s for another theme command's hold,
# and applies the recorded package again when its content or that font
# changed, over whatever the theme file holds. `theme list` reports a theme
# file still holding those bytes unmodified.
# Apply also makes the package's remembered background image, else its
# first, the current one: the ${XDG_STATE_HOME:-~/.local/state}/vgshell/background
# symlink and backgrounds.json beside it, and clears every output's own
# image. `theme background next` and `previous` hold the same lock and move
# to the applied package's next or previous image, remembering it. `theme
# background set` holds it too and shows one image from a package's
# backgrounds/ or from ${XDG_CONFIG_HOME:-~/.config}/vgshell/backgrounds/, a
# regular file never reached through a symlink below that folder; with
# --screen it is that Hyprland output's own image, and with --every-screen
# every output's own image is cleared. `theme background list`
# names the applied package's images, and with --all every package's and
# the user folder's: docs/architecture/themes.md § Backgrounds.
# `--json` prints the list, the apply's result `{ state, shell, targets,
# theme, reason }`, the follow's, that result with its `follow` outcome,
# the reload's `{ state, targets, reason }`, the background step's
# `{ state, background, theme, path, reason }`, the set's, that result with
# its `screen`, or the background list's `{ state, images, reason }`,
# refusals included, as one JSON line on stdout. `theme` commands never
# contact the shell: docs/architecture/themes.md.
#
# `theme add`, `update` and `remove` follow the plugin rules above for
# ${XDG_CONFIG_HOME:-~/.config}/vgshell/themes/<name>, remove without a
# question: add asks for a missing url as plugin add does, stages the clone,
# lands it under its theme.json name once the theme judge accepts it and
# refuses an occupied name and the reserved `vgs`; when add asked for the
# URL, it prints the apply command and asks whether to run it. A URL on the
# command line keeps add non-interactive. update asks, and takes --yes, as
# plugin update does, refuses a modified checkout and rolls back a version
# the judge refuses, and a version it accepts follows as `theme follow`
# does for that package, its line last and its exit status update's; remove
# deletes only an installed package, never a shipped one.
# Each holds the theme lock while it changes the package, update through
# its question too; a held lock is reason=busy.
# docs/architecture/themes.md.
#
# `theme install` lands the catalog package themes/catalog/<name> the same
# way from a staging directory, under the theme lock, with the marker
# .vgs-catalog.json that makes it a catalog install; it refuses a catalog
# install of that name as reason=installed and anything else there as
# reason=exists. --json prints `{ state, theme, path, shadows, reason }`
# as one line, a refusal's included. update of a catalog install asks nothing: it replaces the
# definition when the catalog's package changed, keeps the package's other
# files, and follows as a git update does. The installed package waits in
# a backup beside themes/ while update swaps it, and add, install, update
# and remove first finish or undo a swap an interruption left, each with a
# `vgshell: recovered: theme=<name> ...` line on stderr. `theme outdated` reports a
# catalog install behind 1 when the catalog's package changed.
# `theme catalog` prints each index entry with `previewPath`, `installed`,
# `definitionUpdate`, `imageryInstalled` and `imageryUpdate`, `--json` as
# `{ entries: [...] }`: docs/architecture/themes.md § Catalog.
#
# `theme wallpapers` is the one catalog command that reaches the network;
# `theme catalog` and `theme install` work offline. It fetches the archive
# a catalog install's index entry pins, over HTTPS only, into
# ${XDG_CACHE_HOME:-~/.cache}/vgshell/theme-assets/, holding download.lock
# there and not the theme lock, checks its size and sha256,
# and unpacks only regular backgrounds/<image> members of at most 128 MiB
# into a staging directory. Under the theme lock it then renames the images
# into the package's backgrounds/ and records the pin in the marker. It
# refuses a backgrounds/ the download did not place as reason=exists,
# since the user's own images belong in ${XDG_CONFIG_HOME:-~/.config}/vgshell/backgrounds/,
# and another archive's images as reason=installed unless --update
# replaces them. It prints progress lines on stderr and `ok wallpapers=<name>
# state=installed|updated|up-to-date images=<n> archive=<sha256>`; --json
# prints `{ state, bytes, total }` lines, then `{ state, theme, wallpapers,
# images, sha256, reason }`, on stdout. VGS_THEME_ASSET_BASE replaces the
# pin's release URL, and takes file:// only in a test run:
# docs/architecture/themes.md § Catalog.
#
# `theme browser-policy install` is the one command that asks for a
# privilege: bin/vgshell-browser-policy installs itself root-owned with one
# NOPASSWD sudoers rule for the calling user, the setup the chromium
# target's hook needs on a home install; the Arch and Fedora packages ship
# both. Apply never prompts: until the setup exists that target is skipped
# with reason=setup-absent, and its hook runs `sudo -n`.
# docs/architecture/themes.md § Targets.
#
# The shell writes the Hyprland layer, one Lua file, to
# ${XDG_STATE_HOME:-~/.local/state}/vgshell/hypr/vgs.lua. `hypr wire` keeps the
# line `pcall(dofile, "<that file>")` first in
# ${XDG_CONFIG_HOME:-~/.config}/hypr/hyprland.lua and `hypr unwire` removes
# it; neither creates hyprland.lua, edits another line or needs a running
# shell, and each prints `ok hypr=wired|unwired|unchanged path=<file>`.
# `hypr remove-bind <file> <line> <key>` takes one whole `hl.bind` line that
# binds a key a VGS shortcut also asks for out of a user file under the
# hypr directory, and `hypr restore-bind <file> <line> <undo>` puts the
# line it printed back, under the same rules: bin/vgshell-hypr-judge.
# `hypr render` asks the running shell to write the layer and run
# `hyprctl reload` now. docs/architecture/hyprland.md.
#
# `--version` and `version` read VERSION beside bin/, one line X.Y.Z, and
# never contact the shell. In a git checkout of this tree, one whose
# top-level directory is the tree itself, they print the describe form
# `vgshell X.Y.Z.r<N>.g<hash>`: X.Y.Z is the newest reachable release tag's,
# a tag v<X.Y.Z>, and N counts the commits since it; with no release tag
# X.Y.Z is VERSION's and N counts every commit. The hash is abbreviated to
# 7 hex digits, more only when 7 are ambiguous. Elsewhere they print
# `vgshell <VERSION>`. `version --json` prints
# `{"version":"<VERSION>","describe":"X.Y.Z.r<N>.g<hash>"}`, describe null
# outside a checkout. Refusals, with nothing on stdout: version=missing or
# version=malformed path=<file>, tag=<tag> reason=not-a-version for a tag
# of v, digits and dots that is not v<X.Y.Z>, and git=describe or
# git=rev-list path=<tree> for a failed git call, a checkout with no commit
# included. docs/architecture/distribution.md.
#
# `self status` names this tree's install method, judged by
# bin/lib/self.js in this order: `checkout` (the top level of its own git
# checkout), `nix` (in the Nix store), `curl` (the directory that
# ${XDG_DATA_HOME:-~/.local/share}/vgshell/current resolves to) and `package`
# (the primary package manager owns VERSION as the package vgshell or vgshell-git).
# A checkout fetches its upstream as `plugin outdated` does and compares
# describe forms; a vgshell-git package compares the commit its version names
# with main's, read by git ls-remote under the same rules; every other
# method compares VERSION, or the vgshell package's version, with the newest
# release's, read from the GitHub API within 10 s. It prints `version=...
# method=... [package=...] current=... latest=... behind=true|false`, or
# with --json one line `{ version, method, package, current, latest,
# behind, error }`. A step that fails is the report's error, its keyed
# refusal line, with latest and behind null and exit 0. `self update`
# fast-forwards a checkout with no diff and no question, refusing a
# modified one as `plugin update` does; replaces a curl install by the
# newest release under ${XDG_DATA_HOME:-~/.local/share}/vgshell/.self.lock,
# refused self=busy with exit 75 while held, keeping the tree a running
# shell was started from; and refuses a package with
# method=package package=<name> manager=<id> and a Nix install with
# method=nix, naming the step that updates it. After an update it prints
# `ok updated=vgshell from=<old> to=<new>` and restarts a running shell from
# the updated tree, whose restart status becomes the command's, or prints
# shell=not-running; a current tree prints `ok up-to-date=vgshell`.
# docs/architecture/distribution.md § Installation ownership.
#
# `pkg` reads the one package-manager table, shell/Core/PackageManagers.js,
# through bin/vgshell-pkg, which states its output and refusals. It needs no
# running shell. Only `run`, `install` and `remove` change a package, and
# they elevate only in a terminal the user sees: each refuses without one,
# and in a process the shell started: docs/architecture/commands-are-data.md
# § Packages.
#
# `sudo` runs bin/vgshell-sudo-grant, which states its output and refusals.
# `grant` and `revoke` reach its root half through `sudo -N`, which asks
# for the password unless a grant is active, and `grant` asks how long and
# then to confirm, so each needs a terminal; the core floating TUIs
# `core/sudo-grant` and `core/sudo-revoke` run `vgshell sudo grant` and
# `vgshell sudo revoke` in one. `status` reads sudo's own listing and asks
# nothing. The first grant installs the root half. On NixOS, every sudo
# verb writes nothing, reports `skipped=nixos-config`, and `grant` prints
# the `security.sudo.extraRules` snippet:
# docs/architecture/commands-are-data.md § System steps.
#
# `system` runs bin/vgshell-system, which holds the closed table of system
# steps and states its output and refusals. `apply` and `undo` ask one
# question and run sudo, so each needs a terminal; the core floating TUI
# `core/system` runs `vgshell system apply <step>` when a plugin's status
# action names the step: docs/architecture/commands-are-data.md § System steps.
#
# `tui present` hands argv, never a shell string, to `bin/vgshell-tui launch`
# with the full presentation, titled by --title or else by the command's
# file name, and needs no shell running. `tui list` and `tui open` ask the
# running shell, which decides what is listed and launches the TUI; `open`
# prints `ok` once the launcher started, or the shell's refusal, such as
# `tui=<key> reason=launcher-missing` while it found no terminal launcher:
# docs/architecture/commands-are-data.md § Floating TUIs.
#
# `reset` returns VGS to what a fresh install starts from. With mv it moves
# into one new folder, ${XDG_DATA_HOME:-~/.local/share}/vgshell/backups/
# <UTC yyyymmddTHHMMSSZ>/, holding config/, state/, data/ and cache/: every
# entry of ${XDG_CONFIG_HOME:-~/.config}/vgshell but theme.lock, of
# ${XDG_STATE_HOME:-~/.local/state}/vgshell, of ${XDG_CACHE_HOME:-~/.cache}/vgshell,
# and of ${XDG_DATA_HOME:-~/.local/share}/vgshell but backups/ and the curl
# install's own entries, current, X.Y.Z/, git/, .self.lock and
# .self-update-*. A folder made in the same second takes a `.<n>` suffix.
# One of those four directories that is a symlink stays one: the entries
# inside its target move. The runtime directory, hyprland.lua, libsecret,
# the sudo grant, the browser policy and the system steps stay. It then
# writes the folder's path to reset-backup in the state directory and prints
# `ok reset backup=<folder>`. When the moved state named an applied package
# in theme.name, it applies the `vgs` package as `theme apply vgs` does, so
# every themed application draws the defaults; a partial apply exits 3 once
# the shell is back. A state that named none had wired no application and
# gets no apply, as a fresh install has none. It holds the theme lock from before the
# move to the end, waiting up to 10 s while another theme command holds it
# and then refusing reset=vgs reason=busy. A running shell is checked and
# stopped as `restart` does before anything moves, and started again after the apply, with restart's line;
# with none running it prints shell=not-running. The shell started after a
# reset reads reset-backup and offers the restore in its "VGS was reset"
# notice. On a terminal reset lists what
# moves and asks `vgshell: reset VGS? [y/N]` on stderr, and any other answer
# than y or yes refuses declined=reset with nothing moved; --yes skips both.
# With no terminal on stdin and no --yes it asks the running shell to show
# its own question, whose Reset runs `reset --yes`, and prints shell=asked;
# with no shell running either it refuses no-terminal=reset.
#
# `reset restore` moves the current entries into a new backup folder as
# reset does, moves every entry of <backup>, a folder directly under
# backups/ and the newest there when none is named, back into place, and
# removes the emptied folder and a reset-backup that names a folder no longer
# there. When the restored theme.name names a package it applies that
# package again, so every themed application follows the restored
# configuration, and then puts back the restored theme.json, backgrounds.json
# and background, so a hand edit and each screen's image stay. It prints
# `ok restored backup=<folder> aside=<new folder>` and restarts a running
# shell as reset does. It asks `vgshell: restore <name>? [y/N]` as plugin
# remove asks, refusing declined=restore or, with no terminal and no --yes,
# no-terminal=restore. A path outside backups/ is refused
# backup=<path> reason=outside, and an empty backups/ backup=none.
#
# Every refusal prints one keyed line first: `vgshell: refused: <key>=<value>`.
# Exit 0 on success, 1 on a refusal, 2 on a bad invocation, 3 when a theme
# apply, follow or reload is partial (a target failed or its reload stays pending;
# each prints `vgshell: refused: target=<name> reason=<key>`), 4 when restart
# refuses because the session is locked, 75 when another shell holds the
# lock, another theme command holds the theme lock, another self update
# holds its lock or another wallpaper download holds the download lock,
# 69 when the shell is not running, 78 when `run` or `restart` finds a tool below the runtime
# floor. `vgshell --help` prints the short screen from help_text and exits 0.
set -euo pipefail

self="$(readlink -f -- "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}")"
root="$(cd -- "$(dirname -- "$self")/.." && pwd)"
shell_dir="$root/shell"
rt_dir="${XDG_RUNTIME_DIR:-/run/user/$(id -u)}"
lock="$rt_dir/vgshell.lock"
config_home="${XDG_CONFIG_HOME:-$HOME/.config}"

help_text() { # [COMMAND]
  case "${1:-main}" in
    main|"") cat <<'EOF_HELP'
Usage: vgshell <command> [arguments]

Run and control the VGS desktop shell for Hyprland.

Commands:
  start      Start the shell in the background
  run        Run the shell in this terminal, for debugging
  restart    Stop the running shell and start it again
  plugin     List, add, enable, update and remove plugins
  theme      Apply, install and update themes and backgrounds
  doctor     Check that the programs VGS and its plugins need are installed
  self       Check for a VGS update and install it
  reset      Move all VGS settings into a backup and start fresh
  lock       Lock the session
  pkg        Install, remove and upgrade system packages
  sudo       Turn on passwordless sudo for a set time
  system     Apply or undo one-time system setup steps
  hypr       Connect the VGS Hyprland settings to hyprland.lua
  tui        Run a command in a VGS floating terminal

Other commands: pid, ipc, log, version, edit, dictation

Run 'vgshell <command> --help' for the options of one command.
EOF_HELP
      ;;
    start) cat <<'EOF_HELP'
Usage: vgshell start

Start the shell in the background and return after it answers.
EOF_HELP
      ;;
    run) cat <<'EOF_HELP'
Usage: vgshell run

Run the shell in this terminal and hold the instance lock.
EOF_HELP
      ;;
    restart) cat <<'EOF_HELP'
Usage: vgshell restart

Stop the running shell and start it again in the background.
EOF_HELP
      ;;
    pid) cat <<'EOF_HELP'
Usage: vgshell pid

Print the running shell's process ID from the instance lock.
EOF_HELP
      ;;
    ipc) cat <<'EOF_HELP'
Usage: vgshell ipc <qs ipc arguments...>

Send arguments to qs ipc for the running shell.
EOF_HELP
      ;;
    log) cat <<'EOF_HELP'
Usage: vgshell log <qs log arguments...>

Read the running shell's log through qs log.
EOF_HELP
      ;;
    plugin) cat <<'EOF_HELP'
Usage: vgshell plugin <subcommand> [arguments]

Subcommands:
  list                              List plugins and missing requirements
  requirements [--json] <id>        Show one plugin's requirements
  settings <id>                     Print one plugin's settings as JSON
  rescan                            Ask the running shell to scan plugins
  enable <id>                       Enable one plugin
  disable <id>                      Disable one plugin
  validate <dir>                    Validate a plugin manifest
  add [--yes] [<git url>]           Install a plugin from git
  update [--yes] <id>               Fast-forward an installed plugin
  remove [--yes] <id>               Remove an installed plugin
  outdated [--json]                 Check installed plugins for updates
EOF_HELP
      ;;
    theme) cat <<'EOF_HELP'
Usage: vgshell theme <subcommand> [arguments]

Subcommands:
  list [--json]                     List theme packages
  apply [--json] <name>             Apply a theme package
  reload [--json]                   Run pending reload hooks again
  follow [--json]                   Reapply the theme when its package or the terminal font changed
  background [--json] next|previous Show another background image
  background [--json] set <path> [--screen <output> | --every-screen]
  background [--json] list [--all]  List background images
  add [<git url>]                   Install a theme package from git
  update [--yes] <name>             Fast-forward an installed theme
  remove <name>                     Remove an installed theme
  catalog [--json]                  List catalog themes
  install [--json] <name>           Install one catalog theme
  wallpapers [--json] <name> [--update]
  outdated [--json]                 Check installed themes for updates
  browser-policy install            Install the Chromium color policy
  setup [--json]                    Show one-time theme setup steps
EOF_HELP
      ;;
    hypr) cat <<'EOF_HELP'
Usage: vgshell hypr <subcommand>

Subcommands:
  state     Show whether hyprland.lua loads the VGS Hyprland layer
  wire      Keep the VGS Hyprland layer load line first
  unwire    Remove the VGS Hyprland layer load line
  render    Write the Hyprland layer again and reload Hyprland
  remove-bind <file> <line> <key>
            Remove one of your binds that also takes a VGS key
  restore-bind <file> <line> <undo>
            Put a removed bind line back
EOF_HELP
      ;;
    edit) cat <<'EOF_HELP'
Usage: vgshell edit <path> [line]

Open a file under the user configuration directory in $EDITOR, or xdg-open.
The path is relative to ${XDG_CONFIG_HOME:-$HOME/.config}.
EOF_HELP
      ;;
    pkg) cat <<'EOF_HELP'
Usage: vgshell pkg <subcommand> [arguments]

Subcommands:
  detect [--json]                         Show available package managers
  present <command>...                    Check commands on PATH
  plan install|update|remove|upgrade <manager> [names...]
  check [--json] [--source <manager>]     Count pending updates
  run install|update|remove|upgrade [--manager <id>] [names...]
  run upgrade [--manager <id>] --ignore <name>...
  run update [--manager <id>] <name>... --install <name>...
  install|remove [--manager <id>] [names...]
EOF_HELP
      ;;
    doctor) cat <<'EOF_HELP'
Usage: vgshell doctor [--json]

Check the core and enabled plugin requirements.
EOF_HELP
      ;;
    lock) cat <<'EOF_HELP'
Usage: vgshell lock

Ask the running shell's lock plugin to lock the session.
EOF_HELP
      ;;
    sudo) cat <<'EOF_HELP'
Usage: vgshell sudo <subcommand> [arguments]

Subcommands:
  status             Show whether a passwordless sudo grant is active
  grant [duration]   Grant passwordless sudo for minutes, or indefinitely
  revoke             End the active grant now
  install            Install the root half of the grant
  uninstall          Remove every grant and the root half
EOF_HELP
      ;;
    dictation) cat <<'EOF_HELP'
Usage: vgshell dictation setup

Copy dictation defaults when no voxtype config exists, enable the speech
engine through the system setup step, and download the configured model.
EOF_HELP
      ;;
    system) cat <<'EOF_HELP'
Usage: vgshell system <subcommand> [arguments]

Subcommands:
  status [--json]      Show each one-time system step's state
  apply <step>         Show and apply a system step
  undo <step>          Undo only what VGS changed for the step
EOF_HELP
      ;;
    tui) cat <<'EOF_HELP'
Usage: vgshell tui <subcommand> [arguments]

Subcommands:
  present [--title <text>] [--size default|wide|tall] -- <argv...>
  list       List floating terminal commands
  open <key> Open one listed floating terminal command
EOF_HELP
      ;;
    self) cat <<'EOF_HELP'
Usage: vgshell self <subcommand> [arguments]

Subcommands:
  status [--json]    Show install state and available update
  update             Update VGS and restart the shell
EOF_HELP
      ;;
    reset) cat <<'EOF_HELP'
Usage: vgshell reset [--yes]
       vgshell reset restore [--yes] [<backup>]

Options:
  --yes      Do not ask before moving files or restoring a backup

Subcommands:
  restore    Restore a backup, or the newest backup when absent
EOF_HELP
      ;;
    version) cat <<'EOF_HELP'
